WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.200
Take a look at this simple setup.

2
00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:09.610
We have a standard process attempting to
read a file that contains exactly eight

3
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.880
characters of data, A through H.

4
00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:17.030
For our first test, the process uses the
open system call twice on the same file,

5
00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:19.300
assigning two different file descriptors.

6
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:23.500
If we read four bytes from the
first descriptor, we get ABCD.

7
00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:27.710
If we read four bytes from the second
descriptor, we get the exact same text.

8
00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:31.280
The final output is ABCD, ABCD.

9
00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:34.500
Now, let's change just one line of code.

10
00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:39.010
Instead of opening the file a second
time, we will use the dup system call to

11
00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.720
duplicate the first descriptor.

12
00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.890
The first read still gets ABCD, but the
second read picks up exactly where the first

13
00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.640
one left off, reading EFGH.

14
00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:52.080
Our final output is now ABCD, EFGH.

15
00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:56.700
Why does changing one system call
completely alter how the file is read?

16
00:00:56.920 --> 00:01:00.300
To find the answer, we have to
look below the privilege boundary.

17
00:01:00.680 --> 00:01:03.260
Let's examine the operating
system's architecture.

18
00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:07.180
Above the privilege boundary is
user mode, where our program runs.

19
00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:11.130
Below the line is kernel mode, where the
kernel strictly controls access to the

20
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:15.900
hardware. When you read a file, your
process relies on three hidden tables.

21
00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.120
The first is the descriptor table.

22
00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:21.860
The kernel maintains one unique
descriptor table for every process.

23
00:01:22.380 --> 00:01:24.960
Next is the system-wide open file table.

24
00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:29.250
This table tracks the current offset, the
exact byte position where the very next

25
00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.500
read or write operation will happen.

26
00:01:31.960 --> 00:01:35.340
Finally, at the bottom, we have
the in-memory inode table.

27
00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.780
This is the single active representation
of the file's metadata loaded in RAM.

28
00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:44.650
When a program calls open twice, the kernel
creates two completely separate entries

29
00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:49.210
in the open file table. Each entry gets
its own independent offset, starting at

30
00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:53.240
zero. That is why both of those
descriptors read the start of the file.

31
00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:55.700
The dup system call works differently.

32
00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:58.600
It simply copies the pointer
in the descriptor table.

33
00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:02.820
Both descriptors now point to the exact
same entry in the open file table.

34
00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:07.200
Because they share a single open file
description, they share the offset.

35
00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:10.980
When one read advances the
position, the other sees the change.

36
00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:15.810
This is the exact mechanism your shell relies
on when redirecting error and standard

37
00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.000
outputs into a single log file.

38
00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:22.550
Notice what happens to this structure if
a file has an inode but no actual data

39
00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:26.520
blocks on the disk. This
is a FIFO, or named pipe.

40
00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:31.910
Instead of writing to a drive, a FIFO
routes data through a 64-kilobyte buffer

41
00:02:31.960 --> 00:02:35.320
located entirely inside
the kernel's memory.

42
00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.630
Because this pipe sits in memory, it
enforces strict rules to keep data

43
00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:45.100
synchronized. If a process calls open to
read a FIFO, it will intentionally block.

44
00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:48.940
The kernel makes it wait until another
process opens the pipe for writing.

45
00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:52.700
Once the connection is made,
writing is heavily regulated.

46
00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:57.540
On Linux, any write of 4096
bytes or less is atomic.

47
00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.870
The kernel slots these blocks in cleanly,
guaranteeing that messages from different

48
00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:03.440
writers never mix.

49
00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:09.330
And when the last writer closes its
descriptor, the reader simply receives a zero,

50
00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:11.260
indicating the end of the file.

51
00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:16.390
The kernel manages complex inter-process
communication using the exact same three-table

52
00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:19.880
architecture, even when the
disk is completely absent.

53
00:03:20.260 --> 00:03:22.720
Let's test these rules with
a practical constraint.

54
00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:27.730
What happens if we create a FIFO inside a
network shared folder and try to use it as

55
00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:30.220
a chat pipe between two different laptops?

56
00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:35.050
The connection fails. A shared network
folder only broadcasts the name of the file

57
00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:38.800
to other machines. It cannot share
the underlying kernel memory.

58
00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:43.430
When each laptop opens that network path,
its local operating system simply creates

59
00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:46.440
its own isolated 64-kilobyte buffer.

60
00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:48.880
The two machines never actually connect.

61
00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.150
A file is a live object, managed actively
and exclusively within the boundaries of a

62
00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:56.620
local machine's kernel. Let's review.

63
00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.210
When a process calls dup, which kernel
table entry is shared to keep the offset in

64
00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:07.430
sync? The open file description. Why
can't a FIFO connect two different

65
00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:09.960
computers over a shared network drive.

66
00:04:10.340 --> 00:04:15.480
Because the pipe exists entirely in the
local machine's kernel, not on the disk.

67
00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:23.020
Now open animations A3.2 and A3.3
in the course hub and run lab TD2.
